Parameterise the demo driver so it can score a GraFlag dataset and emit the
scores.

Upstream's example/Demo.cpp hardcodes the DARPA paths under SOLUTION_DIR,
selects a core by editing two commented lines, and has its score-writing block
commented out -- so it can only benchmark the one dataset the repository ships,
and it prints an AUC without emitting the scores that AUC came from. GraFlag
publishes the scores a method computed rather than a number it printed, so the
driver has to take paths and write the vector.

The algorithm is untouched. MIDAS is header-only: NormalCore.hpp,
RelationalCore.hpp and FilteringCore.hpp *are* the method, this patch does not
go near them, and they are used with exactly the constructors upstream defines.
example/Demo.cpp is a driver, which is all it was before.

Two behaviours change deliberately, and both are recorded in README.md:
  - the seed comes in as an argument instead of time(nullptr), so re-running an
    experiment reruns the same experiment;
  - a short or malformed data file is now an error. fopen() succeeding says
    nothing about shape, and upstream ignores fscanf's return value by compiler
    flag (-Wno-unused-result), so a truncated file left the tail of the arrays
    uninitialised and scored garbage silently.

The AUROC print is kept from upstream's Demo, now optional on a label path.
GraFlag records it so the AUC the method measured on its own scores can be
checked against the one the evaluator computes from the published vector --
two numbers that disagree mean the published scores are not the scored ones.

